{
  "id": "ss-elementary",
  "title": "Elementary Signals",
  "description": "Steps, impulses, ramps, pulses and exponentials: the building blocks of every signal.",
  "reference": "Phillips §2.3–2.5, app. D",
  "study": [
    {
      "title": "Unit step and ramp",
      "body": "The unit step $u(t)$ is 0 for $t<0$ and 1 for $t>0$: it switches something on at $t=0$. Its running integral is the unit ramp $r(t) = t\\,u(t)$. Conversely $dr/dt = u(t)$. Shifted versions switch on later: $u(t - t_0)$ jumps at $t = t_0$.",
      "formula": "r(t) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{t}u(\\tau)\\,d\\tau = t\\,u(t)",
      "plot": {
        "x": {
          "min": -1,
          "max": 3,
          "label": "t",
          "ticks": [
            -1,
            0,
            1,
            2,
            3
          ]
        },
        "y": {
          "min": -0.3,
          "max": 2.5,
          "label": "",
          "ticks": [
            0,
            1,
            2
          ]
        },
        "curves": [
          {
            "f": "step(x)",
            "color": "primary"
          },
          {
            "f": "max(0,x)",
            "color": "blue",
            "dash": true
          }
        ],
        "labels": [
          {
            "x": -0.8,
            "y": 1.25,
            "text": "u(t)",
            "color": "primary"
          },
          {
            "x": 0.15,
            "y": 2.2,
            "text": "r(t) = t·u(t)",
            "color": "blue"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Unit impulse",
      "body": "$\\delta(t)$ is zero for $t \\ne 0$ and has unit area; think of a pulse of width $\\varepsilon$ and height $1/\\varepsilon$ with $\\varepsilon \\to 0$. It is drawn as an arrow (here a stem) whose height shows the **area**. Rules:\n- $\\delta(t) = du/dt$\n- $x(t)\\,\\delta(t - t_0) = x(t_0)\\,\\delta(t - t_0)$\n- $\\delta(at) = \\delta(t)/|a|$\n- Discrete: $\\delta[n]$ = 1 at $n=0$ only, $\\delta[n] = u[n] - u[n-1]$.",
      "formula": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(t)\\,\\delta(t - t_0)\\,dt = x(t_0)",
      "plot": {
        "x": {
          "min": -1,
          "max": 3,
          "label": "t",
          "ticks": [
            -1,
            0,
            1,
            2,
            3
          ]
        },
        "y": {
          "min": -0.3,
          "max": 1.5,
          "label": "",
          "ticks": [
            0,
            1
          ]
        },
        "curves": [
          {
            "f": "(1)*(x-(1)+0.012)/(2*0.012)",
            "from": "(1)-0.012",
            "to": "(1)+0.012",
            "color": "primary"
          }
        ],
        "points": [
          {
            "x": 1,
            "y": 1,
            "guides": false
          }
        ],
        "labels": [
          {
            "x": 1.15,
            "y": 1.2,
            "text": "δ(t − 1), area 1",
            "color": "primary"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Rectangular pulse",
      "body": "A pulse of height $A$ from $t_1$ to $t_2$ is a step **on** at $t_1$ minus a step that switches it **off** at $t_2$. The centred unit pulse $\\mathrm{rect}(t)$ is 1 for $|t| < \\tfrac12$. Multiplying any signal by a pulse cuts out a time window.",
      "formula": "A\\left[u(t - t_1) - u(t - t_2)\\right]",
      "plot": {
        "x": {
          "min": -1,
          "max": 5,
          "label": "t",
          "ticks": [
            -1,
            0,
            1,
            2,
            3,
            4,
            5
          ]
        },
        "y": {
          "min": -0.5,
          "max": 3,
          "label": "",
          "ticks": [
            0,
            1,
            2,
            3
          ]
        },
        "curves": [
          {
            "f": "2*(step(x-1)-step(x-3))"
          }
        ],
        "labels": [
          {
            "x": 1.2,
            "y": 2.5,
            "text": "2[u(t−1) − u(t−3)]",
            "color": "primary"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Real and complex exponentials",
      "body": "$e^{at}$ grows for $a>0$ and decays for $a<0$ (time constant $\\tau = -1/a$). The complex exponential $e^{j\\omega_0 t}$ has magnitude 1 and rotates around the unit circle, period $2\\pi/\\omega_0$. With $s = \\sigma + j\\omega$, $\\mathrm{Re}\\{e^{st}\\} = e^{\\sigma t}\\cos(\\omega t)$: a sinusoid inside an exponential envelope, damped for $\\sigma<0$.",
      "formula": "e^{st} = e^{\\sigma t}\\left[\\cos(\\omega t) + j\\sin(\\omega t)\\right]",
      "plot": {
        "x": {
          "min": 0,
          "max": 4,
          "label": "t",
          "ticks": [
            0,
            1,
            2,
            3,
            4
          ]
        },
        "y": {
          "min": -1.2,
          "max": 1.2,
          "label": "",
          "ticks": [
            -1,
            0,
            1
          ]
        },
        "curves": [
          {
            "f": "exp(-0.5*x)*cos(2*pi*x)"
          },
          {
            "f": "exp(-0.5*x)",
            "color": "grey",
            "dash": true
          },
          {
            "f": "-exp(-0.5*x)",
            "color": "grey",
            "dash": true
          }
        ],
        "labels": [
          {
            "x": 2.6,
            "y": 0.75,
            "text": "envelope",
            "color": "grey"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Euler's formula",
      "body": "Euler links sinusoids and exponentials. Every real sinusoid is the sum of two counter-rotating complex exponentials, or the real part of one: $A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi) = \\mathrm{Re}\\{Ae^{j\\phi}e^{j\\omega t}\\}$, where $Ae^{j\\phi}$ is the phasor.\n$\\cos\\theta = \\frac{e^{j\\theta} + e^{-j\\theta}}{2}$, $\\quad\\sin\\theta = \\frac{e^{j\\theta} - e^{-j\\theta}}{2j}$",
      "formula": "e^{j\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + j\\sin\\theta"
    },
    {
      "title": "Building signals from steps and ramps",
      "body": "Read a piecewise signal from left to right.\n- At every **jump** of height $h$ at $t_k$, add $h\\,u(t - t_k)$.\n- At every **change of slope** $\\Delta m$ at $t_k$, add $\\Delta m\\, r(t - t_k)$.\nThe trapezoid below rises with slope 1 on $[0, 1]$, is flat until 3 and falls to 0 at 4, so it is $r(t) - r(t-1) - r(t-3) + r(t-4)$.",
      "plot": {
        "x": {
          "min": -1,
          "max": 5,
          "label": "t",
          "ticks": [
            -1,
            0,
            1,
            2,
            3,
            4,
            5
          ]
        },
        "y": {
          "min": -0.3,
          "max": 1.5,
          "label": "",
          "ticks": [
            0,
            1
          ]
        },
        "curves": [
          {
            "f": "max(0,x)-max(0,x-1)-max(0,x-3)+max(0,x-4)"
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ],
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "ss-elementary-1",
      "title": "Steps, impulses and ramps",
      "kind": "learn",
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "i1",
          "type": "info",
          "title": "The unit step",
          "body": "The unit step $u(t)$ is 0 before $t = 0$ and 1 after. It models a switch closing. $A\\,u(t - t_0)$ jumps by $A$ at $t = t_0$; adding and subtracting shifted steps builds any piecewise-constant signal.",
          "formula": "u(t) = \\begin{cases} 0 & t < 0 \\\\ 1 & t > 0 \\end{cases}",
          "plot": {
            "x": {
              "min": -1,
              "max": 3,
              "label": "t",
              "ticks": [
                -1,
                0,
                1,
                2,
                3
              ]
            },
            "y": {
              "min": -0.3,
              "max": 2.5,
              "label": "",
              "ticks": [
                0,
                1,
                2
              ]
            },
            "curves": [
              {
                "f": "step(x)",
                "color": "primary"
              },
              {
                "f": "max(0,x)",
                "color": "blue",
                "dash": true
              }
            ],
            "labels": [
              {
                "x": -0.8,
                "y": 1.25,
                "text": "u(t)",
                "color": "primary"
              },
              {
                "x": 0.15,
                "y": 2.2,
                "text": "r(t) = t·u(t)",
                "color": "blue"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "q1",
          "type": "numeric",
          "prompt": "Find $x({{t0}})$ for $x(t) = {{A}}\\,u(t - {{t1}}) - {{B}}\\,u(t - {{t2}})$.",
          "answer": "A*step(t0-t1) - B*step(t0-t2)",
          "unit": "",
          "explanation": "At $t = {{t0}}$: $u({{t0}} - {{t1}}) = {{step(t0-t1)}}$ and $u({{t0}} - {{t2}}) = {{step(t0-t2)}}$, so $x = {{A}}\\cdot{{step(t0-t1)}} - {{B}}\\cdot{{step(t0-t2)}}$ = {{A*step(t0-t1) - B*step(t0-t2)}}. A step is 1 once its argument is positive.",
          "params": {
            "A": [
              2,
              3,
              4,
              5
            ],
            "B": [
              2,
              3,
              6
            ],
            "t1": [
              1,
              2
            ],
            "t2": [
              3,
              4
            ],
            "t0": [
              0.5,
              1.5,
              2.5,
              3.5,
              4.5
            ]
          },
          "absTolerance": 0.01
        },
        {
          "id": "i2",
          "type": "info",
          "title": "The unit ramp",
          "body": "Integrating the step gives the ramp $r(t) = t\\,u(t)$: zero before 0, then rising with slope 1. Going the other way, differentiating lowers the signal by one level: ramp $\\to$ step $\\to$ impulse.",
          "formula": "r'(t) = u(t), \\qquad u'(t) = \\delta(t)"
        },
        {
          "id": "q2",
          "type": "matchPairs",
          "prompt": "Match each operation to its result.",
          "pairs": [
            [
              "$\\frac{d}{dt}u(t)$",
              "$\\delta(t)$"
            ],
            [
              "$\\int_{-\\infty}^{t}u(\\tau)d\\tau$",
              "$r(t)$"
            ],
            [
              "$\\frac{d}{dt}r(t)$",
              "$u(t)$"
            ],
            [
              "$\\int_{-\\infty}^{t}r(\\tau)d\\tau$",
              "$\\tfrac12 t^2u(t)$"
            ]
          ],
          "explanation": "Each integration raises the power of $t$ by one: $\\delta \\to u \\to r = t\\,u \\to \\tfrac12t^2u$. Differentiation goes back down the chain; the jump of $u$ at 0 differentiates into $\\delta(t)$."
        },
        {
          "id": "i3",
          "type": "info",
          "title": "The unit impulse",
          "body": "The impulse $\\delta(t)$ is an infinitely narrow pulse with **area 1** at $t = 0$. Multiplying a signal by $\\delta(t - t_0)$ keeps only its value at $t_0$, so integrating \"sifts out\" that one value:",
          "formula": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)\\,\\delta(t - t_0)\\,dt = x(t_0)",
          "plot": {
            "x": {
              "min": -1,
              "max": 3,
              "label": "t",
              "ticks": [
                -1,
                0,
                1,
                2,
                3
              ]
            },
            "y": {
              "min": -0.3,
              "max": 1.5,
              "label": "",
              "ticks": [
                0,
                1
              ]
            },
            "curves": [
              {
                "f": "(1)*(x-(1)+0.012)/(2*0.012)",
                "from": "(1)-0.012",
                "to": "(1)+0.012",
                "color": "primary"
              }
            ],
            "points": [
              {
                "x": 1,
                "y": 1,
                "guides": false
              }
            ],
            "labels": [
              {
                "x": 1.15,
                "y": 1.2,
                "text": "δ(t − 1), area 1",
                "color": "primary"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "q3",
          "type": "numeric",
          "prompt": "Evaluate the integral (limits $-\\infty$ to $\\infty$).",
          "answer": "v1",
          "unit": "",
          "explanation": "",
          "params": {
            "c": [
              2,
              3,
              4,
              5
            ],
            "t0": [
              1,
              2,
              3
            ]
          },
          "derived": {
            "v1": "t0^2 + c",
            "v2": "1 - c*t0"
          },
          "absTolerance": 0.01,
          "ask": [
            {
              "prompt": " $\\int (t^2 + {{c}})\\,\\delta(t - {{t0}})\\,dt$",
              "answer": "v1",
              "explanation": "The impulse sits where $t - {{t0}} = 0$, i.e. $t = {{t0}}$. Sifting: $({{t0}})^2 + {{c}}$ = {{v1}}."
            },
            {
              "prompt": " $\\int ({{c}}t + 1)\\,\\delta(t + {{t0}})\\,dt$",
              "answer": "v2",
              "explanation": "$\\delta(t + {{t0}})$ sits where $t + {{t0}} = 0$, i.e. at $t = {{-t0}}$, not at {{t0}}. Sifting: ${{c}}\\cdot({{-t0}}) + 1$ = {{v2}}."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "q4",
          "type": "trueFalse",
          "prompt": "$\\int_{-1}^{1} e^{t}\\,\\delta(t - 2)\\,dt = e^{2}$",
          "answer": false,
          "explanation": "The impulse is at $t = 2$, outside the interval $[-1, 1]$. Over that interval the integrand is zero everywhere, so the integral is **0**. Sifting only works when $t_0$ lies inside the limits."
        },
        {
          "id": "i4",
          "type": "info",
          "title": "Rectangular pulses",
          "body": "Switch on at $t_1$, switch off at $t_2$: a pulse is the difference of two shifted steps. Multiplying a signal by such a pulse keeps only the part inside the window.",
          "formula": "A\\left[u(t - t_1) - u(t - t_2)\\right]",
          "plot": {
            "x": {
              "min": -1,
              "max": 5,
              "label": "t",
              "ticks": [
                -1,
                0,
                1,
                2,
                3,
                4,
                5
              ]
            },
            "y": {
              "min": -0.5,
              "max": 3,
              "label": "",
              "ticks": [
                0,
                1,
                2,
                3
              ]
            },
            "curves": [
              {
                "f": "2*(step(x-1)-step(x-3))"
              }
            ],
            "labels": [
              {
                "x": 1.2,
                "y": 2.5,
                "text": "2[u(t−1) − u(t−3)]",
                "color": "primary"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "q5",
          "type": "multipleChoice",
          "prompt": "Which expression describes the pulse shown?",
          "options": [
            "${{A}}\\left[u(t - {{t1}}) - u(t - {{t1+w}})\\right]$",
            "${{A}}\\left[u(t + {{t1}}) - u(t + {{t1+w}})\\right]$",
            "${{A}}\\left[u(t - {{t1+w}}) - u(t - {{t1}})\\right]$",
            "${{A}}\\left[u(t - {{t1}}) - u(t - {{w}})\\right]$"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The pulse switches on at $t = {{t1}}$ (step $+{{A}}\\,u(t - {{t1}})$) and off at $t = {{t1+w}}$ (step $-{{A}}\\,u(t - {{t1+w}})$). The second step uses the **end time** {{t1+w}}, not the width {{w}}. Reversing the order would give a negative pulse; $u(t + t_1)$ would switch on at $t = -{{t1}}$.",
          "params": {
            "A": [
              2,
              3,
              4
            ],
            "t1": [
              1,
              2
            ],
            "w": [
              1,
              2,
              3
            ]
          },
          "require": "abs(w-t1)",
          "plot": {
            "x": {
              "min": -1,
              "max": 6,
              "label": "t",
              "ticks": [
                -1,
                0,
                1,
                2,
                3,
                4,
                5,
                6
              ]
            },
            "y": {
              "min": -0.5,
              "max": 5,
              "label": "",
              "ticks": [
                0,
                1,
                2,
                3,
                4,
                5
              ]
            },
            "curves": [
              {
                "f": "A*(step(x-t1)-step(x-t1-w))"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
